{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons (often ~+0.3 to +3 EUR, occasionally larger). Apply an upward level shift to the whole path.",
    "Underestimation grows with horizon: the gap tends to widen after ~day 7–10 and is largest in the back half of the 30d window. Use a horizon-dependent uplift (small for days 1–5, larger for days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: when the market is in an upswing, the forecast follows but with insufficient slope (lags the rally). Add extra positive drift when recent returns are positive and volatility is rising.",
    "Post-spike mean-reversion bias: the model reverts too quickly after strength, leading to pronounced underpricing in later days (e.g., forecasts falling toward low/mid-60s while outcomes stay high-60s). Reduce mean reversion strength in bullish regimes.",
    "Tail risk miss: late-window dips in the forecast (sharp drops) are often not realized (true path stays elevated). Cap downward extrapolations unless supported by recent downside momentum."
  ]
}
